12:28.290 --> 12:31.950
hopefully it works on Stevie's machine and it does.

12:31.950 --> 12:33.300
So that's the basic idea.

12:33.300 --> 12:34.470
We're working on branches.

12:34.470 --> 12:36.770
That allows us to collaborate more freely.

12:36.770 --> 12:39.210
We can experiment, try radical changes.

12:39.210 --> 12:42.330
We treat the main or the master branch or whatever branch,

12:42.330 --> 12:46.170
some branch as the the important golden child

12:46.170 --> 12:47.730
branch that we don't wanna screw up.

12:47.730 --> 12:50.760
So we make feature branches, we make all sorts of branches.

12:50.760 --> 12:52.950
It doesn't just have to be for a new feature

12:52.950 --> 12:55.230
but we make branches that we work on

12:55.230 --> 12:57.540
often people just call them feature branches.

12:57.540 --> 12:59.490
The thing that we have neglected to talk about

12:59.490 --> 13:03.180
with working on feature branches is the inevitable merging

13:03.180 --> 13:04.320
of those feature branches.

13:04.320 --> 13:06.000
Or at least some of them.

13:06.000 --> 13:08.460
We need to merge them into the master or main branch.

13:08.460 --> 13:11.490
And how we do this is up for discussion.

13:11.490 --> 13:13.490
That's what we're gonna talk about next.
